diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/compute-slurm.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/compute-slurm.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..f3434515a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/compute-slurm.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+## Configure Slurm
+
+Similarly, we can configure Slurm and import the cryptographic key that is
+required by the *munge* authentication library to be available on every host in
+the resource management pool, issue the following:
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+```bash
+# Configure Slurm server in the overlay (using "configless" option)
+# using a tag in a template file (slurmd.ww)
+wwctl overlay import --parents nodeconfig \
+ /opt/ohpc/pub/examples/slurm/slurmd.ww /etc/sysconfig/slurmd.ww
+
+# Set the value of the slurmctld tag to the $sms_ip for the nodes profile.
+wwctl profile set --yes nodes --tagadd slurmctld="${sms_ip}"
+
+# Configure munge
+wwctl overlay import --parents nodeconfig /etc/munge/munge.key
+wwctl overlay chown nodeconfig /etc/munge/munge.key \
+	"$(id -u munge)" "$(id -g munge)"
+wwctl overlay chown nodeconfig /etc/munge "$(id -u munge)" "$(id -g munge)"
+wwctl overlay chmod nodeconfig /etc/munge 0700
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/deploy-add-nodes.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/deploy-add-nodes.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..7c905d96e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/deploy-add-nodes.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+## Register Compute Notes for Provisioning
+
+Nodes can be registered for provisioning using the following:
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+```bash
+for ((i=0; i<num_computes; i++)) ; do
+    wwctl node add --image={{ baseos }} --profile=nodes --netname=default \
+        --ipaddr="${c_ip[$i]}" --hwaddr="${c_mac[$i]}" "${c_name[i]}"
+done
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+Optionally define IPoIB network settings (required if planning to mount
+Lustre/BeeGFS over IB)
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_if_set enable_ipoib -->
+```bash
+for ((i=0; i<num_computes; i++)) ; do
+	wwctl node set --yes "${c_name[$i]}" --netname=ib --netdev=ib0 \
+		--ipaddr="${c_ipoib[$i]}" --netmask="${ipoib_netmask}"
+done
+```
+<!-- ohpc_fi -->
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+Finally, rebuild the image to incorporate any optional packages installed into
+the chroot during customization, then rebuild the overlays and update the
+Warewulf configuration.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Rebuild image and overlays after customization -->
+```bash
+# Rebuild image to include any customization chroot changes
+wwctl image build {{ baseos }}
+
+# build the overlays for all the nodes
+wwctl overlay build
+
+# Update Warewulf configure
+wwctl configure --all
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/finalize-provisioning.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/finalize-provisioning.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..a9fdc1506
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/finalize-provisioning.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,101 @@
+## Finalizing Provisioning Configuration
+
+Warewulf provisions a node with an image then customizes it with overlays. This
+section highlights creation of the node image and overlays, followed by the
+registration of desired compute nodes.
+
+### Two-Stage Provisioning (optional)
+
+Warewulf optionally supports two-stage provisioning to ramfs or disk on the
+compute nodes. The nodes are still stateless, but the image is copied to disk
+during the provisioning process and is reprovisioned on every boot.  This can be
+useful for cases when the compute node images are large, for example GPU
+drivers, or the compute nodes have limited memory.
+
+To configure two-stage provisioning, install Dracut and ignition (ignition can
+also be used to provision swap and local storage (see Warewulf documentation for
+details) on the compute node.  The example below provisions to ramfs, which is
+the default.  To provision to disk, see the next section.  To aid in debugging,
+it is helpful to add `rd.shell` to the kernel arguments.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+```bash
+## Add rd.shell to kernel arguments in the "default" profile.
+yq -i '.nodeprofiles.default.kernel.args += ["rd.shell"]' \
+  /etc/warewulf/nodes.conf
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_if [[ ${enable_dracut} -eq 1 ]] -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Two-Stage provision to ramfs -->
+```bash
+## Install Dracut in the image
+wwctl image exec --build=false {{ baseos }} -- /usr/bin/dnf install -y \
+  warewulf-ohpc-dracut
+
+## Configure Dracut. Note the leading and trailing spaces
+# in the quotes in "add_dracutmodules"
+echo 'hostonly="no"' > "$CHROOT"/etc/dracut.conf.d/wwinit.conf
+echo 'add_dracutmodules+=" wwinit "' \
+  >> "$CHROOT"/etc/dracut.conf.d/wwinit.conf
+
+## Enable Dracut boot for compute nodes that use the "nodes" profile
+wwctl profile set --yes nodes --tagadd IPXEMenuEntry=dracut
+```
+<!-- ohpc_fi -->
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+Optionally, configure the compute node to provision to disk.  Two stage
+provisioning (previous step) must also be enabled.  The compute node will use,
+**and erase**, the disk specified by `node_disk`.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_if [[ ${enable_dracut} -eq 1 && ${enable_todisk} -eq 1 ]] -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Configure two-stage provision-to-disk -->
+```bash
+## Install disk tools
+wwctl image exec --build=false {{ baseos }} -- /usr/bin/dnf install -y \
+  ignition gdisk
+
+## Add ignition Dracut module.
+echo 'add_dracutmodules+=" ignition "' \
+  >> "$CHROOT"/etc/dracut.conf.d/wwinit.conf
+
+## Create the target "rootfs" partition and filesystem
+wwctl profile set --yes nodes \
+  --diskname "${node_disk}" --diskwipe \
+  --partname rootfs --partcreate --partnumber 1 \
+  --fsname rootfs --fswipe --fsformat ext4 --fspath /
+
+## Enable provision-to-disk for compute nodes that use the "nodes" profile
+wwctl profile set nodes --yes --root=/dev/disk/by-partlabel/rootfs
+```
+<!-- ohpc_fi -->
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+### Build Image and Overlays
+
+The bootstrap image includes the runtime kernel and associated modules, as well
+as some simple scripts to complete the provisioning process.  We explicitly
+rebuild Dracut and the image at the end because rebuilding the image is disabled
+in earlier steps. It is good practice to rebuild the overlays when after
+configuration changes.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Build Dracut -->
+```bash
+## Build the Dracut initramfs
+wwctl image exec --build=false {{ baseos }} -- /usr/bin/dracut --force \
+  --regenerate-all
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Assemble bootstrap image -->
+```bash
+# Build image
+wwctl image build {{ baseos }}
+wwctl overlay build
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/post-add-user.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/post-add-user.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..b10c82ba1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/post-add-user.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+## Add User
+
+We will now add a new user to the cluster.  This will be used later to run a
+test job.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+```bash
+useradd -m test
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+Warewulf installs a utility on the compute nodes to automatically synchronize
+overlay files from the provisioning server at one minute intervals. To rebuild
+the overlay, run the following:
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+```bash
+wwctl overlay build
+```
+<!-- ohpc_comment Wait for wwclient to pull overlays (pulls every 60 seconds) -->
+<!-- ohpc_command sleep 90 -->
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+After re-syncing to notify Warewulf of file modifications made on the *head
+node*, it should take approximately one minute for the changes to propagate.
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/warewulf-install.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/warewulf-install.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..461f84e4a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/warewulf-install.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+## Install Warewulf on *Head Node*
+
+To add support for provisioning services, we install the Warewulf provisioning
+system packages. Then the main Warewulf configuration files are edited to
+reflect the environment. The `yq` package is also installed to aid in
+configuring Warewulf `yaml` files.
+
+<!-- FIXME: remove semanage when fixed in Warewulf -->
+<!-- FIXME: remove /var/lib/tftpboot when fixed in Warewulf -->
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Add baseline OpenHPC -->
+```bash
+# Make sure required variables are set
+: "${sms_eth_internal:?ERROR: sms_eth_internal not set}"
+: "${internal_network:?ERROR: internal_network not set}"
+
+# Install Warewulf
+{{ pkg_install }} warewulf-ohpc yq
+
+# Create the tftpboot directory (not created by dhcpd)
+install -d -m 0755 /var/lib/tftpboot
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+If SELinux is enabled, configure the appropriate file context for the `tftpboot` directory:
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_if [[ "$(getenforce 2>/dev/null)" != "Disabled" ]] -->
+```bash
+# Add public_content_t type to tftpboot directory and contents
+{{ pkg_install }} policycoreutils-python-utils
+semanage fcontext -a -t public_content_t "/var/lib/tftpboot(/.*)?"
+restorecon -R -v /var/lib/tftpboot
+```
+<!-- ohpc_fi -->
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/warewulf-setup.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/warewulf-setup.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..f721132fe
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf/warewulf-setup.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+## Complete basic Warewulf setup for *head node*
+
+At this point, all of the packages necessary to use Warewulf on the *head node*
+should be installed. Next, we need to update the configuration to allow Warewulf
+to work with {{ distro_full_name }}, and to support local provisioning using a
+second private interface (refer to Figure 1).
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Complete basic Warewulf setup for head node -->
+```bash
+# Edit the warewulf.conf file to use appropriate interface and settings
+yq -i '.ipaddr = "'"${sms_ip}"'"' /etc/warewulf/warewulf.conf
+yq -i '.netmask = "'"${internal_netmask}"'"' /etc/warewulf/warewulf.conf
+yq -i '.network = "'"${internal_network}"'"' /etc/warewulf/warewulf.conf
+yq -i '.dhcp.template = "static"' /etc/warewulf/warewulf.conf
+yq -i 'del(.dhcp["range start"]) | del(.dhcp["range end"])' \
+	/etc/warewulf/warewulf.conf
+
+# Edit the nodes.conf to mount /opt on boot
+sed -i "s/defaults,noauto,nofail,ro/defaults,nofail,ro/" \
+	/etc/warewulf/nodes.conf
+
+# Turn on debugging messages
+yq -i '.nodeprofiles.default.kernel.args -= ["quiet"]' \
+	/etc/warewulf/nodes.conf
+
+# Enable and start the warewulfd service before using wwctl.
+systemctl enable --now warewulfd
+
+# Create a new "nodes" profile and inherit the "default" profile
+wwctl profile add nodes --profile default --comment "Nodes profile"
+
+# Create a new "nodeconfig" overlay to store node
+# configuration files and use the syncuser overlay.
+wwctl overlay create nodeconfig
+wwctl profile set --yes nodes --system-overlays nodeconfig \
+	--runtime-overlays syncuser
+
+# Set default network configuration
+wwctl profile set -y nodes --netname=default --netdev="${eth_provision}"
+wwctl profile set -y nodes --netname=default --netmask="${internal_netmask}"
+wwctl profile set -y nodes --netname=default --gateway="${ipv4_gateway}"
+wwctl profile set -y nodes --netname=default --nettagadd=DNS="${dns_servers}"
+
+# Configuring Warewulf will restart/enable relevant services to support provisioning
+wwctl configure --all
+
+# Generate ssh keys (usually generated on login)
+bash /etc/profile.d/ssh_setup.sh
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/add-hosts.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/add-hosts.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..699de75d0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/add-hosts.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+## Register Compute Nodes for Provisioning
+
+In preparation for provisioning, we define the desired network settings for the
+example compute nodes and restart the `dhcp` service. Note the use of variable
+names for the desired compute hostnames, node IPs, and MAC addresses which
+should be modified to accommodate local settings and hardware.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Add hosts to cluster -->
+```bash
+# Set provisioning interface as the default networking device
+echo "GATEWAYDEV=${eth_provision}" > /tmp/network.$$
+wwsh -y file import /tmp/network.$$ --name network
+wwsh -y file set network --path /etc/sysconfig/network --mode=0644 --uid=0
+
+# Add nodes to Warewulf data store
+for ((i=0; i<num_computes; i++)) ; do
+    wwsh -y node new "${c_name[$i]}" \
+        --ipaddr="${c_ip[$i]}" --hwaddr="${c_mac[$i]}" -D "${eth_provision}"
+done
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+```bash
+# Additional step required if desiring to use predictable network interface
+# naming schemes (e.g. en4s0f0). Skip if using eth# style names.
+export kargs="${kargs} net.ifnames=1,biosdevname=1"
+wwsh provision set --postnetdown=1 "${compute_regex}"
+```
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Add hosts to cluster (Cont.) -->
+```bash
+# Define provisioning image for hosts
+wwsh -y provision set "${compute_regex}" \
+    --vnfs={{ baseosshort }} \
+    --bootstrap=$(uname -r) \
+    --files=dynamic_hosts,passwd,group,shadow,{% if is_slurm %}munge.key,{% endif %}network
+
+# Restart dhcp / update PXE
+systemctl restart dhcpd
+wwsh pxe update
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-create-image.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-create-image.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..fb4d4ae43
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-create-image.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+## Define *Compute* Image for Provisioning
+
+With the provisioning services enabled, the next step is to define and
+customize a system image that can subsequently be used to provision one or more
+*compute* nodes.
+
+### Build Initial Base OS Image
+
+The OpenHPC build of Warewulf includes specific enhancements enabling support
+for {{ distro_full_name }}. The following steps illustrate the process to build
+a minimal, default image for use with Warewulf. We begin by defining a
+directory structure on the *head node* that will represent the root filesystem
+of the compute node. The default location for this example is
+`/opt/ohpc/admin/images/{{ baseosshort }}`.
+
+::: {.tip}
+Warewulf is configured by default to access an external repository during the
+`wwmkchroot` process. If the head node cannot reach the public {{ distro_full_name }}
+mirrors, set the `${BOS_MIRROR}` environment variable to your desired repo
+location prior to running `wwmkchroot`.
+:::
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Create compute image for Warewulf -->
+```bash
+# Define chroot location
+export CHROOT=/opt/ohpc/admin/images/{{ baseosshort }}
+{% if is_sles %}
+# Create chroot directory structure
+mkdir -p -m 755 $CHROOT
+mkdir -m 755 $CHROOT/dev
+mknod -m 666 $CHROOT/dev/zero c 1 5
+
+# Build initial chroot image
+wwmkchroot -v {{ baseos }} $CHROOT
+
+# Enable OpenHPC repo in chroot
+cp -p /etc/zypp/repos.d/OpenHPC*.repo $CHROOT/etc/zypp/repos.d
+
+# Import GPG keys for chroot repository usage
+zypper -n --root $CHROOT --no-gpg-checks --gpg-auto-import-keys refresh
+{% else %}
+# Override default OS repository (optional) - set BOS_MIRROR to desired repo
+# export YUM_MIRROR=${BOS_MIRROR}
+
+# Build initial chroot image
+wwmkchroot -v {{ baseos }} $CHROOT
+
+# Enable OpenHPC and EPEL repos inside chroot
+{{ pkg_install_chroot }} epel-release
+cp -p /etc/yum.repos.d/OpenHPC*.repo $CHROOT/etc/yum.repos.d
+{% endif %}
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-customize.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-customize.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..26f942578
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-customize.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+## Customize System Configuration
+
+Prior to assembling the image, it is advantageous to perform additional
+customization within the chroot environment. The following steps add a local
+*ssh* key created by Warewulf to support remote access, and enable NFS mounting
+of the `$HOME` file system and the public OpenHPC install path
+(`/opt/ohpc/pub`) hosted by the *head node*.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Customize system configuration -->
+```bash
+# Initialize warewulf database and ssh_keys
+wwinit database
+wwinit ssh_keys
+
+# Add NFS client mounts of /home and /opt/ohpc/pub to base image
+echo "${sms_ip}:/home /home nfs nfsvers=4,nodev,nosuid 0 0" \
+    >> $CHROOT/etc/fstab
+echo "${sms_ip}:/opt/ohpc/pub /opt/ohpc/pub nfs nfsvers=4,nodev 0 0" \
+    >> $CHROOT/etc/fstab
+
+# Export /home and OpenHPC public packages from head node
+echo "/home *(rw,no_subtree_check,fsid=10,no_root_squash)" >> /etc/exports
+echo "/opt/ohpc/pub *(ro,no_subtree_check,fsid=11)" >> /etc/exports
+
+systemctl restart nfs-server
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-import-files.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-import-files.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..517d7348f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-import-files.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+## Import Files
+
+The Warewulf system includes functionality to import arbitrary files from the
+provisioning server for distribution to managed hosts. This is one way to
+distribute user credentials to *compute* nodes. To import local file-based
+credentials, issue the following:
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Import files -->
+```bash
+wwsh file import /etc/passwd
+wwsh file import /etc/group
+wwsh file import /etc/shadow
+{% if is_slurm %}
+# Import munge key required by Slurm authentication
+wwsh file import /etc/munge/munge.key
+{% endif %}
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-install-ohpc.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-install-ohpc.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..3e31824ab
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-install-ohpc.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+## Add OpenHPC Components
+
+Next, we add additional components to include resource management client
+services, NTP support, and other additional packages to support the default
+OpenHPC environment. This process augments the chroot-based install performed
+by `wwmkchroot` and will access the Base OS and OpenHPC repositories to resolve
+package install requests.
+
+We begin by installing a few common base packages:
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Add OpenHPC base components to compute image -->
+```bash
+# Copy DNS configuration from head node
+cp -p /etc/resolv.conf $CHROOT/etc/resolv.conf
+{% if is_sles %}
+# Import GPG keys for chroot repository usage
+zypper -n --root $CHROOT --no-gpg-checks --gpg-auto-import-keys refresh
+
+# Copy credential files to ensure consistent uid/gids for package installs
+cp /etc/passwd /etc/group $CHROOT/etc
+{% endif %}
+# Install compute node base meta-package
+{{ pkg_install_chroot }} ohpc-base-compute
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+Now, we can include additional required components to the compute instance
+including resource manager client, NTP, and development environment modules
+support.
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-install-scheduler.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-install-scheduler.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..3ff130532
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/compute-install-scheduler.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+{% if is_slurm %}
+<!-- ohpc_comment Add Slurm and other components to compute instance -->
+```bash
+# Copy credential files to ensure consistent uid/gids for slurm/munge
+cp /etc/passwd /etc/group $CHROOT/etc
+
+# Add Slurm client support meta-package and enable munge and slurmd
+{{ pkg_install_chroot }} ohpc-slurm-client
+chroot $CHROOT systemctl enable munge
+chroot $CHROOT systemctl enable slurmd
+
+# Register Slurm server with computes (using "configless" option)
+echo SLURMD_OPTIONS="--conf-server ${sms_ip}" > $CHROOT/etc/sysconfig/slurmd
+{% elif is_openpbs %}
+<!-- ohpc_comment Add OpenPBS and other components to compute instance -->
+```bash
+# Add OpenPBS client support
+{{ pkg_install_chroot }} openpbs-execution-ohpc
+sed -i "s/PBS_SERVER=\S\+/PBS_SERVER=${sms_name}/" $CHROOT/etc/pbs.conf
+echo "PBS_LEAF_NAME=${sms_name}" >> /etc/pbs.conf
+chroot $CHROOT opt/pbs/libexec/pbs_habitat
+sed -i "s/\\\$clienthost \S\+/\\\$clienthost ${sms_name}/" \
+    $CHROOT/var/spool/pbs/mom_priv/config
+echo "\$usecp *:/home /home" >> $CHROOT/var/spool/pbs/mom_priv/config
+chroot $CHROOT systemctl enable pbs
+{% endif %}
+
+# Add Network Time Protocol (NTP) support
+{{ pkg_install_chroot }} chrony
+{% if is_sles %}
+chroot $CHROOT systemctl enable chronyd
+{% endif %}
+# Identify head node as local NTP server
+echo "server ${sms_ip} iburst" >> $CHROOT/etc/chrony.conf
+
+# Add kernel drivers
+{% if is_sles %}
+{{ pkg_install_chroot }} kernel-default
+{% else %}
+{{ pkg_install_chroot }} kernel-$(uname -r)
+{% endif %}
+# Include modules user environment
+{{ pkg_install_chroot }} lmod-ohpc
+{% if is_sles %}
+# Enable ssh access
+chroot $CHROOT systemctl enable sshd.service
+
+# Remove default hostname to allow WW to provision network names
+mv $CHROOT/etc/hostname $CHROOT/etc/hostname.orig
+
+# Provide udev rules to enable /dev/ipath access for InfiniPath devices
+cp /opt/ohpc/pub/examples/udev/60-ipath.rules $CHROOT/etc/udev/rules.d/
+{% endif %}
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/finalize-provisioning.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/finalize-provisioning.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..87e76ea39
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/finalize-provisioning.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+## Finalize Provisioning Configuration
+
+Warewulf employs a two-stage boot process: a bootstrap image initializes the
+process, and a Virtual Node File System (VNFS) capsule contains the full system
+image.
+
+### Assemble Bootstrap Image
+
+The bootstrap image includes the runtime kernel and associated modules, as well
+as some simple scripts to complete the provisioning process.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Assemble bootstrap image -->
+```bash
+# Optionally include drivers from kernel updates; add virtio_net for VM support
+export WW_CONF=/etc/warewulf/bootstrap.conf
+echo "drivers += updates/kernel/" >> $WW_CONF
+echo "modprobe += virtio_net" >> $WW_CONF
+
+# Build bootstrap image
+wwbootstrap $(uname -r)
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+### Assemble VNFS Image
+
+With the local site customizations in place, the following step assembles a
+VNFS capsule from the chroot environment defined for the *compute* instance.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Assemble VNFS -->
+```bash
+wwvnfs --chroot $CHROOT
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/post-add-user.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/post-add-user.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..efe7ce6c0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/post-add-user.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+## Add User
+
+We will now add a new user to the cluster. This will be used later to run a
+test job.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+```bash
+useradd -m test
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+Warewulf installs a utility on the compute nodes to automatically synchronize
+known files from the provisioning server at five minute intervals. With the
+addition of the new "test" user, we need to update the Warewulf database to
+incorporate the additions:
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+```bash
+wwsh file resync passwd shadow group
+```
+<!-- ohpc_comment Wait for wwgetfiles to pull updates (runs every 5 minutes) -->
+<!-- ohpc_command sleep 2 -->
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+After re-syncing to notify Warewulf of file modifications made on the *head
+node*, it should take approximately 5 minutes for the changes to propagate.
+However, you can also manually pull the changes from compute nodes via:
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_if_set enable_warewulf_getfiles -->
+```bash
+pdsh -w "${compute_prefix}[1-${num_computes}]" /warewulf/bin/wwgetfiles
+```
+<!-- ohpc_fi -->
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/warewulf3-install.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/warewulf3-install.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..ccb29cac9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/warewulf3-install.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
+## Install Warewulf on *Head Node*
+
+To add support for provisioning services, we install the Warewulf provisioning
+system packages. Then the main Warewulf configuration files are edited to
+reflect the environment.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Add baseline OpenHPC -->
+```bash
+# Make sure required variables are set
+: "${sms_eth_internal:?ERROR: sms_eth_internal not set}"
+
+# Install Warewulf
+{{ pkg_install }} ohpc-warewulf
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/warewulf3-setup.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/warewulf3-setup.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..30eb01d5f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/provisioner/warewulf3/warewulf3-setup.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+## Configure Warewulf
+
+With Warewulf installed, the next step is to update the configuration to allow
+Warewulf to work with {{ distro_full_name }} and to support local provisioning
+using a second private interface.
+
+::: {.tip}
+By default, Warewulf is configured to provision over the `eth1` interface. The
+steps below update this setting to use the interface specified by
+`${sms_eth_internal}`.
+:::
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Complete basic Warewulf setup for head node -->
+```bash
+# Configure Warewulf provisioning to use desired internal interface
+sed -i "s/device = eth1/device = ${sms_eth_internal}/" \
+    /etc/warewulf/provision.conf
+{% if is_sles %}
+# Configure DHCP server to use desired internal interface
+sed -i "s/^DHCPD_INTERFACE=\S\+/DHCPD_INTERFACE=${sms_eth_internal}/" \
+    /etc/sysconfig/dhcpd
+
+# Configure Warewulf to use the SLES tftp location
+sed -i "s/#tftpdir = \/var\/lib\//tftpdir = \/srv\//" \
+    /etc/warewulf/provision.conf
+
+# Configure Warewulf to use the correct certificate bundle
+sed -i "s/cacert =.*/cacert = \/etc\/ssl\/ca-bundle.pem/" \
+    /etc/warewulf/provision.conf
+
+# Update Warewulf http configuration to use SLES Apache module paths
+export MODFILE=/etc/apache2/conf.d/warewulf-httpd.conf
+sed -i "s#modules/mod_perl.so\$#/usr/lib64/apache2/mod_perl.so#" $MODFILE
+sed -i "s#modules/mod_version.so\$#/usr/lib64/apache2/mod_version.so#" $MODFILE
+{% endif %}
+{% if is_sles %}
+# Enable Apache mod_rewrite module (needed for warewulf)
+a2enmod mod_rewrite
+# Restart/enable relevant services to support provisioning
+systemctl enable mysql.service
+systemctl restart mysql
+systemctl enable apache2.service
+systemctl restart apache2
+{% else %}
+# Configure MariaDB to handle large VNFS images
+sed -i "/\[mysqld\]/a max_allowed_packet=32M" /etc/my.cnf.d/mariadb-server.cnf
+sed -i "s/^# database chunk size.*/database chunk size = 16777216/" \
+    /etc/warewulf/database.conf
+systemctl enable mariadb
+systemctl restart mariadb
+systemctl enable httpd.service
+systemctl restart httpd
+{% endif %}
+systemctl enable dhcpd.service
+systemctl enable tftp.socket
+systemctl start tftp.socket
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/install.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/install.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..b9f2d45f5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/install.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@
+## Add OpenPBS on Head Node
+
+OpenHPC provides multiple options for distributed resource management. The
+following command adds the {{ scheduler_name }} workload manager server
+components to the chosen *head node*. Note that client-side components will be
+added to the corresponding compute image in a subsequent step.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Add resource management services on head node -->
+```bash
+# Install OpenPBS server meta-package
+{{ pkg_install }} openpbs-server-ohpc
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+Other versions of this guide are available that describe installation of other
+resource management systems, and they can be found in the `docs-ohpc` package.
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/startup.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/startup.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..218e1e224
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/startup.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+## Start OpenPBS
+
+In an earlier section, the {{ scheduler_name }} resource manager was installed
+and configured for use on both the *head node* and *compute* node instances.
+With the cluster nodes up and functional, we can now startup the resource
+manager services in preparation for running user jobs.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+<!-- ohpc_comment Resource Manager Startup -->
+```bash
+# Start PBS daemons on head node
+systemctl enable --now pbs
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
+
+In the default configuration, the *compute* hosts have not been added to the
+resource manager. To add the *compute* hosts, execute the following to register
+each host and apply several key global configuration options.
+
+<!-- ohpc_begin -->
+```bash
+# Initialize PBS path
+. /etc/profile.d/pbs.sh
+# Enable user environment propagation (needed for modules support)
+qmgr -c "set server default_qsub_arguments= -V"
+# Enable uniform multi-node MPI task distribution
+qmgr -c "set server resources_default.place=scatter"
+# Enable support for job accounting
+qmgr -c "set server job_history_enable=True"
+# Register compute hosts with PBS
+for host in "${c_name[@]}"; do
+    qmgr -c "create node $host"
+done
+```
+<!-- ohpc_end -->
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/status.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/status.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..5995f0ce9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/status.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+## Check Cluster Status
+
+After this, check status of the nodes within OpenPBS by using the `pbsnodes`
+command. All compute nodes should be in a *free* state. If a node is not
+responding, the following may help diagnose the issue:
+
+```bash
+pbsnodes -a
+```
+
+In case of additional OpenPBS issues, ensure that the `pbs` service is running
+on both the head node and compute nodes, and that the `PBS_SERVER` value in
+`/etc/pbs.conf` on the compute nodes matches the head node hostname.
diff --git a/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/test-job.md.j2 b/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/test-job.md.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..7faf78294
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/install/templates/scheduler/openpbs/test-job.md.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,105 @@
+## Run a Test Job
+
+With the resource manager enabled for production usage, users should now be able
+to run jobs. To demonstrate this, we will add a "test" user on the **head** node
+that can be used to run an example job.
+
+OpenHPC includes a simple "hello-world" MPI application in the
+`/opt/ohpc/pub/examples` directory that can be used for this quick compilation
+and execution. OpenHPC also provides a companion job-launch utility named `prun`
+that is installed in concert with the pre-packaged MPI toolchains. This
+convenience script provides a mechanism to abstract job launch across different
+resource managers and MPI stacks such that a single launch command can be used
+for parallel job launch in a variety of OpenHPC environments. It also provides a
+centralizing mechanism for administrators to customize desired environment
+settings for their users.
+
+## Interactive Execution
+
+To use the newly created "test" account to compile and execute the application
+**interactively** through the resource manager, execute the following (note the
+use of `prun` for parallel job launch which summarizes the underlying native job
+launch mechanism being used):
+
+```bash
+# Switch to "test" user
+su - test
+
+# Compile MPI "hello world" example
+[test@sms ~]$ mpicc -O3 /opt/ohpc/pub/examples/mpi/hello.c
+
+# Submit interactive job request and use prun to launch executable
+[test@sms ~]$ qsub -I -l select=2:mpiprocs=4
+
+[test@c1 ~]$ prun ./a.out
+
+[prun] Master compute host = c1
+[prun] Resource manager = openpbs
+[prun] Launch cmd = mpiexec.hydra -rmk pbs ./a.out
+
+ Hello, world (8 procs total)
+    --> Process #   0 of   8 is alive. -> c1
+    --> Process #   1 of   8 is alive. -> c1
+    --> Process #   2 of   8 is alive. -> c1
+    --> Process #   3 of   8 is alive. -> c1
+    --> Process #   4 of   8 is alive. -> c2
+    --> Process #   5 of   8 is alive. -> c2
+    --> Process #   6 of   8 is alive. -> c2
+    --> Process #   7 of   8 is alive. -> c2
+```
+
+::: {.tip}
+
+The following table provides approximate command equivalences between OpenPBS
+and SLURM:
+
+| **Command** | **OpenPBS** | **SLURM** |
+|---|---|---|
+| Submit *batch* job | qsub [job script] | sbatch [job script] |
+| Request *interactive* shell | qsub -I /bin/bash | salloc |
+| Delete job | qdel [job id] | scancel [job id] |
+| Queue status | qstat -q | sinfo |
+| Job status | qstat -f [job id] | scontrol show job [job id] |
+| Node status | pbsnodes [node name] | scontrol show node [node id] |
+:::
+
+## Batch execution
+
+For batch execution, OpenHPC provides a simple job script for reference (also
+housed in the `/opt/ohpc/pub/examples` directory). This example script can be
+used as a starting point for submitting batch jobs to the resource manager and
+the example below illustrates use of the script to submit a batch job for
+execution using the same executable referenced in the previous interactive
+example.
+
+```bash
+# Copy example job script
+[test@sms ~]$ cp /opt/ohpc/pub/examples/openpbs/job.mpi .
+
+# Examine contents (and edit to set desired job sizing characteristics)
+[test@sms ~]$ cat job.mpi
+#!/bin/bash
+#----------------------------------------------------------
+# Job name
+#PBS -N test
+
+# Name of stdout output file
+#PBS -o job.out
+
+# Total number of nodes and MPI tasks/node requested
+#PBS -l select=2:mpiprocs=4
+
+# Run time (hh:mm:ss) - 1.5 hours
+#PBS -l walltime=01:30:00
+#----------------------------------------------------------
+
+# Change to submission directory
+cd $PBS_O_WORKDIR
+
+# Launch MPI-based executable
+prun ./a.out
+
+# Submit job for batch execution
+[test@sms ~]$ qsub job.mpi
+5.master
+```
